WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.560
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.240
Sometimes it’s hard to figure out what to
make a video about, and so you call your brother and you ask him,

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.740
and he reminds you that he’s really stressed out right now, maybe a little bit sad also,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.180
and he just wants a video about humans being joyful and amazing.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:17.500
So John, here is a video about people being joyful because their fruits and vegetables are amazing.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:23.280
Humans have been selectively breeding plants since there was agriculture, resulting in some pretty fantastic transformations.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.880
Like check out this ancient ancestor of the carrot.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:26.740
No, thanks.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.700
And this seedy little green banana ball.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.590
I think I’d rather not, thank you.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.760
And my very favorite agricultural transformation:

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:41.720
This spindly little grass seed here was apparently useful enough that we kept breeding it and breeding it and breeding it until finally we got freaking corn!

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:42.220
What?!

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.240
And we remain very good at this.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:49.160
So good that we just do it for fun now, and we end up with ludicrously large fruits and vegetables.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.880
And I like them, and I love to see the people who are so happy about them.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.350
Let’s start out with the smallest of the
big.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.000
The largest apple ever—only 4 pounds!

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.720
Also on the list of surprisingly small large
things is the largest ever potato: only 8 pounds.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:10.420
So if you extend that to sweet potatoes, Lebanese farmer Khalil Semhat would have something to say about it with his 25-pound sweet potato.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:17.300
In 1998, Alaskan farmer and mechanical engineer John Evans grew the largest ever cabbage, over 76 pounds.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.560
And until this year, John Evans was also the record-holder for the world’s largest carrot.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:28.520
But this year, the title was taken Minnesotan Chris Qualley with his 22-pound carrot that is, frankly, upsetting to look at.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.000
This here is Tim Saint.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.520
He did not grow the largest zucchini, but
I did very much like that picture of him holding it.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.539
Likewise, look at this happy woman who has grown the world’s largest pineapple.

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:39.539
It’s Christine McCallum.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.240
Thank you.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.280
And of course, as long as we’re talking
about happy people holding their giant vegetables,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.100
here’s Peter Glazebrook holding his world’s largest onion like a baby.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:47.600
Oh yeah.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.780
Peter Glazebrook and his onion are the cutest thing.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:52.540
I love them.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:59.100
Trees and bushes will often have a structural problem growing giant fruits, which is why this world’s largest tomato is being held up by some pantyhose.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:07.060
But weirdly enough, there’s a variety of
lemon called the nine pound lemon tree that grows nine-pound lemons or more, and somehow it manages this just fine.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:15.080
And there are a few nine pound lemon trees at Epcot in Orlando, where I think the world’s largest lemon was harvested—15 pounds.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.220
Now beating out all this stuff by a lot are
the gourds.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.120
Not the band (there is a band called The Gourds).

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.440
Melons, squashes, cucumbers are all members of the gourd family, and gourds can grow very big.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:31.020
The world’s largest watermelon, grown by
Chris Kent in Tennessee, weighed in at over 350 pounds.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.540
And remember that the largest thing on this list so far was that 76-pound cabbage.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.020
This is a big watermelon!

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.020
But it is nothing compared to the biggest
pumpkin.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:47.240
Now, here’s the situation: The pumpkin is
the biggest fruit or vegetable, so if you grow the largest pumpkin, you have grown the largest fruit.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:52.060
John, I didn’t know this until I started
researching this video, but there are giant pumpkin festivals all over the world.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:03:01.260
So the fight for who has the largest pumpkin is hard fought, and in 2016, the winner beat the previous record by over 300 pounds!

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.940
Mathias Willemijns of Belgium hit it out of the park.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.720
It’s 2600 pounds.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.400
Look at this man! He is a star!

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:16.020
But these festivals tend to be held around
October 10th, which I know is an important day for other reasons,

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.920
so we don’t know if Mathias’s pumpkin is gonna get beat out in 2017.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.500
John, the World Championship of Pumpkins Weigh-Off is this Sunday.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.780
I don’t know about you, but that’s gonna
take over all of the anxiety I have over anything else.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.140
That’s all I’ll be thinking about is this
wonderful pumpkin championship.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.140
Nothing else is gonna stress me out.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.380
Just be at peace. You’ll be fine. Your book is great. People will love it.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:46.160
And John, I will see on Sunday, when I fly
to New York City on the very day of the European Championship of Pumpkins Weigh-Off!

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.280
Best of luck to all the pumpkin competitors at all of the pumpkin festivals.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.060
I think that you and all of your pumpkins
are just great.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:56.300
In the end, you can turn them into a canoe
and there is actually a canoe race.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.520
It’s just pumpkin canoes.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.440
Humans! Wow!

